koli</surname><given-names>CO</given-names></name><name><surname>Akah</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>Onuoha</surname><given-names>NJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Okoye</surname><given-names>TC</given-names></name><name><surname>Nwoye</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Nworu</surname><given-names>CS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title><italic>Acanthus montanus</italic>: an experimental evaluation of the antimicrobial, anti-inflammatory and immunological properties of a traditional remedy for furuncles</article-title><source>BMC Compl Altern Med</source><year>2008</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>1472</fpage><lpage>6882</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18538006</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ma</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Kou</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhu</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Yan</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Liu-Shen-Wan, a traditional Chinese medicine, improves survival in sepsis induced by cecal ligation and puncture via reducing TNF-&#x003b1; levels, MDA content and enhancing macrophage phagocytosis</article-title><source>Int Immunopharmacol</source><year>2006</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>1355</fpage><lpage>1362</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16782549</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.intimp.2006.03.003</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Breitschwerdt</surname><given-names>EB</given-names></name><name><surname>Loar</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Hribernik</surname><given-names>TN</given-names></name><name><surname>Grath</surname><given-names>RK</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Hypoglycemia in four dogs with sepsis</article-title><source>J American Vet Med Assoc</source><year>1981</year><volume>178</volume><fpage>1072</fpage><lpage>1076</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lang</surname><given-names>CH</given-names></name><name><surname>Dobrescu</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sepsis-induced increases in glucose uptake by macrophage-rich tissues persist during hypoglycemia</article-title><source>Metabolism</source><year>1991</year><volume>40</volume><fpage>585</fpage><lpage>593</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1678134</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0026-0495(91)90048-2</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maitra</surname><given-names>SR</given-names></name><name><surname>Wojnar</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name><name><surname>Lang</surname><given-names>CH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Alterations in tissue glucose uptake during the hyperglycemic and hypoglycemic phases of sepsis</article-title><source>Shock</source><year>2000</year><volume>13</volume><fpage>379</fpage><lpage>385</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10807013</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>